window.AIBEMOCK={"n":4,"title":"Mock Test 4","theme":"Full syllabus","kind":"Full-length paper","blurb":"A fourth full-length paper in the same pattern. By now your subject-wise weak spots should be showing, so use the analysis to decide what to revise.","minutes":210,"neg":0,"pass":{"General / OBC":45,"SC / ST / PwD":40},"asOn":"19 November 2026","q":[{"s":"Jurisprudence","q":"Roscoe Pound described the function of law as","o":["the union of primary and secondary rules in a mature legal system","the command of the sovereign, backed by a sanction against disobedience","the expression of the Volksgeist, or the spirit, of the nation concerned","social engineering, balancing the competing interests in society"],"a":3,"e":"Pound, the leader of the sociological school, regarded law as a tool of social engineering, aimed at satisfying the maximum of wants with the minimum of friction, through the recognition of individual, public and social interests."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"The competence of a child as a witness depends on","o":["his having attained the age of twelve years on the date of the testimony","his guardian having given consent for him to testify in the case at hand","his capacity to understand the questions and to give rational answers","his having taken an oath in the form that has been prescribed by the court"],"a":2,"e":"There is no minimum age for a witness. A child is competent if the court is satisfied that he can understand questions and give rational answers (Dattu Ramrao Sakhare v State of Maharashtra, 1997). The court may need to take care, as children are susceptible to tutoring."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under Section 35(7) of the BNSS, for an offence punishable with imprisonment of less than three years, the arrest of a person who is infirm or above sixty years of age requires prior permission of","o":["the Superintendent of Police of the district in which the arrest is made","an officer not below the rank of Deputy Superintendent of Police","the Magistrate having jurisdiction over the concerned police station area","the Director General of Police of the State in which the arrest is made"],"a":1,"e":"This is a new safeguard in BNSS. For offences punishable with less than three years, the police cannot arrest an infirm person or one above sixty years without prior permission of an officer not below the rank of DSP."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"In Unni Krishnan J.P. v State of Andhra Pradesh (1993), the Supreme Court held that the right to education flowing from Article 21 is available","o":["only to children who study in schools recognised by the Central Government","only to children belonging to the Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes","to every citizen without any limit of age, as a justiciable fundamental right","to every child until fourteen, and later subject to the State's economic capacity"],"a":3,"e":"The Court held that the right to education up to the age of 14 is a fundamental right, and beyond that it is subject to the limits of the State's economic capacity and development under Article 41. Article 21A was inserted by the 86th Amendment in 2002."},{"s":"Administrative Law","q":"In Om Kumar v Union of India (2001), the Supreme Court held that the doctrine of proportionality applies","o":["only to the legislative actions of the Parliament and the State Legislatures","where fundamental rights are affected, while Wednesbury applies in other cases","in all cases of administrative action, replacing Wednesbury review altogether","only to the administrative decisions that are made by a tribunal or court"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held that proportionality is applied by the courts to test administrative action where Articles 14, 19 and 21 are in issue, including the quantum of punishment. Where no fundamental right is involved, the Wednesbury standard continues to apply."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Which of the following is a function of a State Bar Council under Section 6 of the Advocates Act, 1961?","o":["Deciding complaints of misconduct against advocates on its roll","Laying down the standards of legal education for all law colleges","Designating advocates as Senior Advocates before the High Court","Recognising universities whose law degrees qualify for enrolment"],"a":0,"e":"Section 6 lists the State Bar Council's functions: admitting advocates on its roll, preparing and maintaining the roll, entertaining and determining misconduct cases, safeguarding advocates' rights and interests, and promoting legal reform and welfare schemes. Recognition of universities and standards of legal education are functions of the BCI."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"Under Article 243B, a State is not required to constitute Panchayats at the intermediate level if its population does not exceed","o":["fifty lakh","five lakh","ten lakh","twenty lakh"],"a":3,"e":"The three-tier system of Panchayats (village, intermediate and district) is mandatory except in States with a population of less than 20 lakh, which may have only two tiers."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"A resolution for removal of the Vice-President of India under Article 67(b) can be moved only in","o":["either House, by a majority of the members present and voting","the Lok Sabha, and must be agreed to by the Rajya Sabha","a joint sitting of both Houses, presided over by the Speaker","the Rajya Sabha, and must be agreed to by the Lok Sabha"],"a":3,"e":"The Vice-President is removed by a resolution of the Rajya Sabha passed by a majority of all its then members and agreed to by the Lok Sabha, after fourteen days' notice. He is the ex officio Chairman of the Rajya Sabha."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"The essential difference between theft and criminal breach of trust is that in criminal breach of trust the property is","o":["taken from the possession of the owner without his consent","obtained by putting the owner in fear of injury to himself","entrusted to the accused, who has lawful possession of it","acquired by the accused through a deception practised on the owner"],"a":2,"e":"In theft, the property is taken out of the owner's possession without consent. In criminal breach of trust under Section 316 BNS, the accused is entrusted with the property or has dominion over it, and dishonestly misappropriates or disposes of it."},{"s":"Company Law","q":"Under Section 149(4) of the Companies Act, 2013, a listed public company must have independent directors constituting at least","o":["two of the total number of directors","one-third of the total number of directors","one-half of the total number of directors","one-fourth of the total number of directors"],"a":1,"e":"Section 149(4) requires at least one-third of the total number of directors to be independent directors in a listed public company. Independent directors must satisfy the criteria of independence in Section 149(6)."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Which of the following is prohibited for an advocate under the Bar Council of India Rules?","o":["Accepting a fee from a client for giving a legal opinion in writing","Pleading in a matter in which he is himself pecuniarily interested","Appearing in a court of the State on whose roll he is enrolled","Appearing for an old client in a fresh matter before the same court"],"a":1,"e":"The Rules on duty to the court prohibit an advocate from acting or pleading in a matter in which he is himself pecuniarily interested. He must also not appear in a matter in which he is a witness, unless he withdraws."},{"s":"Public International Law","q":"Under the Vienna Convention on Consular Relations, 1963, consular officers enjoy immunity from jurisdiction","o":["in respect of civil acts only, but not for criminal acts","in respect of all acts, whether official or private, as diplomats do","only in respect of acts performed in the exercise of consular functions","in respect of no act, since consuls have no immunity at all"],"a":2,"e":"Consular immunity is functional, covering acts in the exercise of consular functions, in contrast to the wider immunity of diplomatic agents under the Vienna Convention on Diplomatic Relations, 1961. Consular officers may also be arrested for grave crimes under Article 41."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Section 96(3) CPC, an appeal","o":["lies from a consent decree only with the leave of the High Court concerned","does not lie from a decree passed by the court with consent of the parties","lies from a consent decree only to the Supreme Court of India directly","lies on the merits from a consent decree, to the High Court concerned"],"a":1,"e":"A consent decree is in the nature of a contract between the parties, and no appeal lies from it. A party may challenge it only by showing that the consent was obtained by fraud or was otherwise vitiated, through a suit or an application before the same court."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Which of the following is exempt from attachment and sale in execution of a decree under Section 60 CPC?","o":["A motor car owned by the judgment-debtor","Shares held by the judgment-debtor in a company","Agricultural land belonging to an agriculturist judgment-debtor","The books of account of the judgment-debtor"],"a":3,"e":"Section 60 exempts necessary wearing apparel, cooking vessels, beds and bedding, tools of artisans, books of account, a mere right to sue for damages, houses of agriculturists and certain salaries, stipends and pensions. Land, shares and vehicles are attachable."},{"s":"Jurisprudence","q":"Salmond defined a legal right as","o":["a prediction of what the courts will do in practice in each case","a norm that derives its validity from the Grundnorm of the system","a command of the sovereign that is backed by a sanction of the State","an interest recognised and protected by a rule of legal right"],"a":3,"e":"For Salmond, a legal right is an interest the violation of which is a legal wrong, and respect for which is a legal duty. It is an interest-based view, in contrast to the will theory of Austin and the realist view of Holmes."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"A Hindu marriage solemnised in contravention of the age condition in Section 5(iii) of the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955 is","o":["void, unless it is ratified by the parties after they attain majority","valid, though the parties are punishable under Section 18","voidable at the option of either party, within one year","void ab initio, and has no legal effect from the beginning"],"a":1,"e":"Section 5(iii) requires the bridegroom to have completed 21 years and the bride 18 years. A breach does not make the marriage void or voidable under the Act, but attracts punishment under Section 18. The Prohibition of Child Marriage Act, 2006 makes such marriages voidable at the option of the child."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under Section 436 of the CrPC (now Section 478 of the BNSS), a person arrested for a bailable offence who is unable to furnish bail within one week of his arrest is","o":["presumed to have absconded, and is liable to be remanded to judicial custody","presumed to have refused bail, and must stay in judicial custody till trial","presumed to be indigent, and is entitled to be released on his personal bond","liable to be produced before the Sessions Court for a fresh order on bail"],"a":2,"e":"The explanation to Section 436 says that a person who cannot furnish bail within a week of arrest may be presumed to be indigent. The court may then release him on a personal bond without sureties."},{"s":"Taxation","q":"Under Article 266(3) of the Constitution, no money can be withdrawn from the Consolidated Fund of India except","o":["with the approval of the Finance Minister","by a resolution of the Lok Sabha alone","by an order of the President, in writing","under an appropriation made by law"],"a":3,"e":"Article 266(3) says that no moneys out of the Consolidated Fund of India or of a State shall be appropriated except in accordance with law and for the purposes and in the manner provided in the Constitution."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Order XXXII CPC, a suit on behalf of a minor plaintiff is instituted by","o":["the minor himself, after attaining sixteen years","the Collector of the district","his guardian ad litem","his next friend"],"a":3,"e":"A minor sues through a next friend, and is defended through a guardian ad litem. A decree against a minor who has not been properly represented is voidable at his option."},{"s":"Property Law","q":"Under Section 116 of the Transfer of Property Act, 1882, if a lessee remains in possession after the lease ends and the lessor accepts rent, the lease is","o":["terminated, and the lessee becomes a trespasser from that date onwards","renewed from year to year or month to month, as per the purpose of the lease","renewed for a fixed term of eleven months at the same old rate of rent","renewed for a period equal to the original term of the lease that has ended"],"a":1,"e":"This is the doctrine of 'holding over'. If the lessee remains in possession and the lessor accepts rent or otherwise assents, the lease is renewed from year to year, or from month to month, according to the purpose for which the property is leased."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Under the BNS, which of the following statements about robbery is correct?","o":["Robbery requires the use of a deadly weapon by the offender","Every theft is a robbery, if it involves moveable property","Robbery requires at least five persons acting together","Every robbery involves either theft or extortion"],"a":3,"e":"Section 309 BNS (earlier Section 390 IPC) says that in all robbery there is either theft or extortion. Theft becomes robbery if the offender causes or attempts to cause death, hurt or wrongful restraint, or fear of instant death, hurt or wrongful restraint. Five or more persons are needed for dacoity."},{"s":"Land Acquisition Law","q":"Under the RFCTLARR Act, 2013, acquisition of irrigated multi-crop land is permitted","o":["only with the unanimous consent of all the owners of the land concerned","freely, like any other agricultural land that is acquired for a public purpose","never, under any circumstance, even for a public purpose of national importance","only as a last resort, and within limits set by the appropriate Government"],"a":3,"e":"Section 10 restricts the acquisition of irrigated multi-cropped land, and it may be acquired only as a last resort, in demonstrable public interest, subject to limits set by the appropriate Government. The Government must also develop an equal area of wasteland for agriculture."},{"s":"Alternative Dispute Resolution","q":"A settlement agreement arrived at in conciliation, under Section 74 of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996, has the same status and effect as","o":["an ordinary agreement between the parties, enforceable by a suit","an arbitral award on agreed terms under Section 30","a decree of a civil court only after it has been registered","a recommendation which is not enforceable in a court of law"],"a":1,"e":"Under Section 74, the settlement agreement, when signed by the parties, is final and binding, and has the status and effect of an arbitral award on agreed terms. It can be enforced as a decree of the court under Section 36."},{"s":"Jurisprudence","q":"Lon Fuller's 'inner morality of law' includes the principle that laws should","o":["always reflect the opinion of the majority of the people","be uniform in all the States of a federal country","be approved by a body of religious authorities","be promulgated, be clear, and not be retroactive"],"a":3,"e":"Fuller listed eight principles, including generality, promulgation, non-retroactivity, clarity, non-contradiction, possibility of compliance, constancy over time, and congruence between declared rules and official action. A failure on all of them results in no law at all."},{"s":"Land Acquisition Law","q":"Under Section 101 of the RFCTLARR Act, 2013, land that remains unutilised for the purpose for which it was acquired for five years from the date of taking possession must be","o":["returned to the original owners or heirs, or transferred to the State's Land Bank","retained by the Government, which may use it for any other purpose it likes","given to the Collector of the district, who may allot it to any person","sold by public auction, and the proceeds given to the Central Government"],"a":0,"e":"Section 101 provides that, where the land acquired remains unutilised for five years from the date of taking possession, it shall be returned to the original owner or owners, or their legal heirs, or to the Land Bank of the State."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"Under Article 124(4), a Judge of the Supreme Court can be removed by the President only on an address by each House of Parliament supported by","o":["a majority of its total membership and two-thirds of members present and voting","a majority of the total membership of the Lok Sabha alone","two-thirds of the total membership of both Houses sitting together in a joint sitting","a simple majority of the members present and voting in each House of Parliament"],"a":0,"e":"The address must be supported by a majority of the total membership of that House, and a majority of not less than two-thirds of the members present and voting, on the grounds of proved misbehaviour or incapacity. The procedure is governed by the Judges (Inquiry) Act, 1968."},{"s":"Administrative Law","q":"In S.N. Mukherjee v Union of India (1990), the Supreme Court held that administrative authorities exercising quasi-judicial functions must","o":["send the decision to the High Court for confirmation","decide only after consulting the Attorney General","record reasons for their decisions","give the decision in public, in an open hearing"],"a":2,"e":"The Court held that, except where the requirement is dispensed with by statute, an administrative authority exercising judicial or quasi-judicial functions must record reasons. The requirement helps judicial review, and improves the decision-making process."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under Section 8(2) of the Hindu Minority and Guardianship Act, 1956, a natural guardian cannot, without the previous permission of the court, do which of the following?","o":["Deposit the minor's money in a nationalised bank in the minor's name","Pay the minor's school fees out of the funds that belong to the minor","Transfer the minor's immovable property by sale, gift or exchange","Lease the minor's immovable property for a term of three years only"],"a":2,"e":"Section 8(2) requires court permission to mortgage, charge, sell, gift or exchange the minor's immovable property, or lease it for more than five years or beyond a year after the minor attains majority. A transaction without permission is voidable at the minor's instance."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Intoxication is a defence under the BNS only if the intoxicating substance was administered to the accused","o":["without his knowledge, or against his will","with his knowledge, but without his intention to become intoxicated","by a friend, who had no intention to cause any harm","in a quantity that was larger than he had expected to consume"],"a":0,"e":"Voluntary intoxication is no defence, though it may be considered in determining intention where a specific intent is required. Involuntary intoxication, where the substance was administered without knowledge or against the will, is a defence if it rendered the accused incapable of knowing the nature of the act."},{"s":"Interpretation of Statutes","q":"Where two provisions of a statute appear to conflict with each other, courts apply the rule of harmonious construction, which requires that","o":["effect be given to both provisions, so that neither is rendered redundant","the later provision be preferred, and the earlier one be ignored altogether","the provision be applied which favours the person who is claiming relief","the conflict be referred to a larger Bench of the court for resolution"],"a":0,"e":"Under the rule, the court construes the provisions so as to give effect to both, and avoid a conflict. The entire statute must be read as a whole, so that each provision is given a meaning and no part is rendered otiose."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Order XLI Rule 27 CPC, an appellate court may allow additional evidence to be produced if","o":["the opposite party has not objected to its being produced in appeal at all","the party wishes to fill the gaps in the evidence led before the trial court","the evidence was available, but the party did not choose to produce it earlier","it requires the evidence to pronounce judgment, or for any other substantial cause"],"a":3,"e":"Rule 27 allows additional evidence only in limited circumstances: where the trial court refused to admit evidence which ought to have been admitted, or where the appellate court requires the evidence to pronounce judgment or for any other substantial cause."},{"s":"Alternative Dispute Resolution","q":"An application under Section 8 of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996 to refer the parties to arbitration must be made","o":["not later than the date of submitting the first statement on the substance of the dispute","after the issues in the suit have been framed by the court, and not before that","at any time before the judgment is pronounced by the court in the suit","only after the written statement has been filed by the defendant in the suit"],"a":0,"e":"Section 8 requires a judicial authority, if the matter is the subject of an arbitration agreement, to refer the parties to arbitration if a party applies not later than the date of submitting its first statement on the substance of the dispute."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"In Satender Kumar Antil v Central Bureau of Investigation (2022), the Supreme Court directed that ordinary bail applications be disposed of within","o":["four weeks, except where a statutory provision prescribes otherwise","six weeks, except where a statutory provision prescribes otherwise","two weeks, except where a statutory provision prescribes otherwise","one week, except where a statutory provision prescribes otherwise"],"a":2,"e":"The Court directed that bail applications be decided within two weeks, and applications for anticipatory bail within six weeks, with the exception of an intervening application. It also classified offences into four categories for bail purposes and urged Parliament to enact a Bail Act."},{"s":"Contract, Specific Relief & Sale of Goods","q":"Which of the following restraints is valid under Section 27 of the Indian Contract Act, 1872?","o":["A covenant restraining a trader from carrying on any business in India for ten years","A covenant by a seller of goodwill not to carry on a similar business within specified local limits","A covenant restraining a person from marrying, except with the consent of his guardian","A covenant restraining an employee from joining a competitor for five years after he leaves"],"a":1,"e":"Section 27 makes agreements in restraint of trade void, with the exception of the sale of goodwill, where the seller may agree to refrain from carrying on a similar business within specified local limits. Restraints on employees after employment are void, though covenants during the term of employment are valid."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"To raise the presumption of dowry death under Section 113B of the Evidence Act (and the corresponding BSA provision), it must be shown that soon before her death the woman was subjected to cruelty or harassment for, or in connection with,","o":["her failure to bear a child within a period of two years","any demand for dowry","any dispute over the division of ancestral property","any quarrel with her in-laws over domestic matters"],"a":1,"e":"The presumption arises only when the prosecution proves cruelty or harassment 'soon before' death for or in connection with a demand for dowry. The expression 'soon before' requires a proximate and live link between the harassment and the death."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under Section 33 of the Indian Succession Act, 1925, when a Christian male dies intestate leaving a widow and lineal descendants, the widow is entitled to","o":["one-third of the estate, and the descendants take the remaining two-thirds","the whole estate, since the lineal descendants take only after her death","one-half of the estate, and the lineal descendants take the other half","one-fourth of the estate, and the lineal descendants take three-fourths"],"a":0,"e":"Where the intestate leaves a widow and lineal descendants, the widow gets one-third and the descendants take two-thirds. If there are no lineal descendants but kindred, the widow takes one-half and the rest goes to the kindred."},{"s":"Interpretation of Statutes","q":"The maxim 'expressio unius est exclusio alterius' means that","o":["general words are limited to things of the same kind as those mentioned","a delegate cannot further delegate the authority that was delegated to him","the express mention of one thing implies the exclusion of others","a special law prevails over a general law on the same subject"],"a":2,"e":"The maxim is an aid to construction. Where a statute expressly mentions certain things, the legislature is taken to have intended to exclude all others, though the maxim must be applied with caution and is not applied where it would defeat the legislative intent."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under Section 94 of the BNSS (earlier Section 91 CrPC), a summons or order to produce a document or other thing can be issued by","o":["a court or any officer in charge of a police station","only the High Court, in a petition under Section 528","only a Sessions Judge, on a written application","only a Magistrate of the first class, in a pending trial"],"a":0,"e":"Section 94 BNSS empowers a court, or an officer in charge of a police station, to summon a person to produce a document or thing necessary for an investigation, inquiry or trial. BNSS extends it to electronic communication devices."},{"s":"Environmental Law","q":"In T.N. Godavarman Thirumulpad v Union of India (1996), the Supreme Court held that the term 'forest' under the Forest (Conservation) Act, 1980 must be understood according to","o":["its dictionary meaning, irrespective of ownership or classification","the definition given in the Indian Forest Act, 1927 alone","the classification made in the revenue records of the State","the opinion of the local Divisional Forest Officer in each case"],"a":0,"e":"The Court gave a wide meaning to 'forest', covering any area recorded as forest in the Government records irrespective of ownership, and any area that fits the dictionary meaning. The Court has since continuously supervised forest governance in India."},{"s":"Labour Law","q":"Under the Code on Wages, 2019, the minimum bonus payable to an eligible employee in respect of an accounting year is","o":["20 per cent of the wages earned during the year","10 per cent of the wages earned during the year","8.33 per cent of the wages earned during the year","5 per cent of the wages earned during the year"],"a":2,"e":"The Code retains the minimum bonus of 8.33% of wages or Rs. 100, whichever is higher, and a maximum of 20%. Employees must have worked for at least thirty days in the accounting year to be eligible."},{"s":"Property Law","q":"Under Section 53 of the Transfer of Property Act, 1882, a transfer of immovable property made with intent to defeat or delay the creditors of the transferor is","o":["voidable at the option of any creditor so defeated or delayed","valid, if the transferee has paid a fair market price","void from the beginning, whatever the consideration paid","valid, if the transfer has been made by a registered deed"],"a":0,"e":"Such a transfer is voidable at the option of the creditors, but it is not voidable against a transferee in good faith and for consideration. The provision protects creditors against fraudulent conveyances."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under Section 24 of the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955, an application for interim maintenance and expenses of the proceeding should, as far as possible, be disposed of within","o":["six months from the date of service of notice on the respondent","ninety days from the date of service of notice on the respondent","thirty days from the date of service of notice on the respondent","sixty days from the date of service of notice on the respondent"],"a":3,"e":"Section 24(2) requires the application to be disposed of, as far as possible, within 60 days of service of notice on the respondent. Either the husband or the wife may claim relief if he or she has no independent income sufficient for support."},{"s":"Alternative Dispute Resolution","q":"Under Section 11(13) of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996, an application for the appointment of an arbitrator should be disposed of expeditiously and an endeavour should be made to dispose of it within","o":["sixty days from the date of service of notice on the opposite party","six months from the date of service of notice on the opposite party","ninety days from the date of service of notice on the opposite party","thirty days from the date of service of notice on the opposite party"],"a":0,"e":"Section 11(13), inserted in 2015, requires an endeavour to dispose of the application within 60 days from the service of notice on the opposite party. The court's role is confined to examining the existence of an arbitration agreement."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"The offence of adultery under Section 497 IPC was struck down as unconstitutional by the Supreme Court in","o":["Joseph Shine v Union of India (2018)","Navtej Singh Johar v Union of India (2018)","Yusuf Abdul Aziz v State of Bombay (1954)","Sowmithri Vishnu v Union of India (1985)"],"a":0,"e":"Joseph Shine held that Section 497 violated Articles 14, 15 and 21 as it treated a woman as the husband's property. Yusuf Abdul Aziz and Sowmithri Vishnu had upheld the provision. The BNS has not retained the offence."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Under the Bar Council of India Rules, an advocate shall not","o":["appear for a client in a matter that is pending before a tribunal","accept a brief from a client who was earlier represented by another advocate","charge a higher fee from a wealthy client than from a client of limited means","stand surety for his client, or certify a surety's soundness, in a case"],"a":3,"e":"The Rules on duty to the client bar an advocate from standing surety, or certifying the soundness of a surety, for his client. He also must not lend money to the client for the purposes of the litigation, though he may agree to pay the court fees on his behalf."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Under the Bar Council of India Rules, which of the following conduct by an advocate is prohibited?","o":["Appearing for a client who was referred to him by a friend","Giving a legal opinion to a client on payment of a proper fee","Canvassing for briefs through circulars, advertisements or touts","Displaying a name-plate of reasonable size outside his office"],"a":2,"e":"Rule 36 prohibits an advocate from soliciting work or advertising, directly or indirectly, by circulars, advertisements, touts or personal communications. A reasonable name-plate is allowed. The Rules were relaxed in 2008 to allow limited information on websites."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Khula, as a mode of dissolution of marriage under Muslim law, is a divorce","o":["effected by arbitration between the two families, without the wife's consent","pronounced by the husband alone, without the consent of the wife concerned","sought by the wife, with her husband's consent, in return for consideration","granted by a court on the husband's petition, without the wife's consent"],"a":2,"e":"In khula, the wife offers to give up her mahr or other property, to be released from the marriage, and the husband accepts. A divorce by mutual consent without a consideration being fixed is called mubarat."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"Entries in books of account regularly kept in the course of business are relevant, but","o":["are admissible only if they are certified by a Chartered Accountant","are not by themselves sufficient to charge any person with liability","are inadmissible in a civil suit, unless the parties have agreed to them","are conclusive proof of the liability of the person who is named in them"],"a":1,"e":"Section 34 of the Evidence Act makes such entries relevant when they refer to a matter the court has to inquire into, but they cannot alone charge a person with liability. Independent evidence is needed to corroborate them."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"A counterclaim set up by a defendant under Order VIII Rule 6A CPC","o":["is treated as a written statement, and cannot be decided by the court at all","must be filed as a separate suit, and cannot be heard in the same suit","can be filed only with the leave of the appellate court that is concerned","is treated as a plaint, and has the effect of a cross-suit decided in the same suit"],"a":3,"e":"A counterclaim has the same effect as a cross-suit, so that the court can pronounce a final judgment on both the original claim and the counterclaim. The cause of action may arise before or after the filing of the suit, but before the time for the written statement expires."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under Section 218 of the BNSS, if the competent authority does not decide on a request for sanction to prosecute a public servant within one hundred and twenty days, the sanction is","o":["deemed to have been accorded","required to be referred to the High Court","deemed to have been refused","deemed to have lapsed altogether"],"a":0,"e":"BNSS prescribes a time-limit of 120 days for the Government to decide on a sanction request. If it does not, sanction is deemed to have been accorded. This overcomes the earlier delay in deciding such requests."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"Under Article 76 of the Constitution, a person can be appointed as Attorney General for India if he is qualified to be appointed as","o":["a Judge of a High Court","a Judge of the Federal Court","a Member of the Union Public Service Commission","a Judge of the Supreme Court"],"a":3,"e":"The Attorney General is appointed by the President, and must be a person qualified to be appointed as a Judge of the Supreme Court. He holds office during the pleasure of the President, and has the right of audience in all courts in India."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Section 34 CPC, the interest which the court may order on the principal sum adjudged from the date of the decree to the date of payment shall not exceed, in a non-commercial transaction,","o":["eighteen per cent per annum","six per cent per annum","twelve per cent per annum","nine per cent per annum"],"a":1,"e":"Section 34(1) provides for interest from the date of decree to payment at a rate the court considers reasonable, not exceeding 6% per annum, except where the liability arises from a commercial transaction, in which case the rate may be higher, up to the contractual rate."},{"s":"Contract, Specific Relief & Sale of Goods","q":"Under Section 70 of the Indian Contract Act, 1872, a person who lawfully does something for another, without intending to do so gratuitously, and the other enjoys the benefit, is entitled to","o":["compensation from the person who has enjoyed the benefit","punitive damages for the inconvenience he has suffered","nothing, since there was no contract between the two persons","only the return of the thing, if it is still in existence"],"a":0,"e":"Section 70 deals with a quasi-contract. The act must be lawful, not intended to be gratuitous, and the other person must enjoy the benefit. The liability is to make compensation or restore the thing."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Section 3(5) of the BNS (earlier Section 34 IPC), which deals with acts done by several persons in furtherance of a common intention, creates","o":["a substantive offence which is punishable by itself","a rule of evidence relating to confessions of the co-accused","a rule that applies only to members of an unlawful assembly","a rule of joint liability, and not a substantive offence"],"a":3,"e":"The provision only lays down the principle of joint liability, which requires a common intention, shared by all, and participation by all in the criminal act. It does not create a separate offence, and a sentence is imposed for the offence committed."},{"s":"Cyber Law","q":"Section 43A of the Information Technology Act, 2000 provides for","o":["compensation for a body corporate's failure to protect sensitive personal data","punishment for cyber terrorism through the use of a computer resource","punishment for identity theft by using another person's password","punishment for hacking into a computer system without the owner's permission"],"a":0,"e":"Section 43A makes a body corporate that possesses, deals with or handles sensitive personal data liable to pay damages by way of compensation if it is negligent in implementing reasonable security practices and thereby causes wrongful loss or gain to any person."},{"s":"Limitation Law","q":"Under Section 17 of the Limitation Act, 1963, where a suit is based on the fraud of the defendant, the period of limitation begins to run from","o":["the date on which the defendant completed the performance of the contract","the date on which the plaintiff instituted a criminal complaint on the fraud","the date on which the fraud was committed by the defendant against the plaintiff","when the plaintiff discovered the fraud, or could with diligence have discovered it"],"a":3,"e":"Section 17 postpones the commencement of limitation in cases of fraud or mistake until discovery, or until the plaintiff could, with reasonable diligence, have discovered the fraud or mistake."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"In Vadivelu Thevar v State of Madras (1957), the Supreme Court held that a conviction can be based on the testimony of a single witness if he is","o":["a person who is related to the victim of the offence","a person who has been declared a hostile witness","wholly reliable, with no need for corroboration","a person who is a police officer in the case"],"a":2,"e":"The Court classified witnesses as wholly reliable, wholly unreliable, and neither wholly reliable nor wholly unreliable. Corroboration is required in the third category only. No particular number of witnesses is required to prove a fact."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"In Sarla Mudgal v Union of India (1995), the Supreme Court held that a Hindu husband who converts to Islam and marries again","o":["remains liable for bigamy, as his first marriage subsists until dissolved","is not liable for bigamy, as his conversion dissolves the first marriage at once","is not liable, since Muslim personal law governs him after his conversion","is liable only if his first wife also converts to Islam along with him"],"a":0,"e":"The Court held that conversion does not automatically dissolve a Hindu marriage, and the second marriage is void under Section 494 IPC (now Section 82 BNS). The Court also called on the Government to implement Article 44 (Uniform Civil Code)."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"Under Section 24 of the Evidence Act (and the corresponding BSA provision), a confession made by an accused is irrelevant in a criminal proceeding if it appears to the court to have been caused by an inducement, threat or promise","o":["unconnected with the charge, made by a relative of the accused","having reference to the charge, proceeding from a person in authority","made by a private person, who has no connection with the charge","made by the accused to himself, before being arrested by the police"],"a":1,"e":"The inducement, threat or promise must have reference to the charge, come from a person in authority, and be such as to give the accused grounds, which appear reasonable to the court, for supposing that by confessing he would gain an advantage or avoid an evil in reference to the proceedings."},{"s":"Property Law","q":"Under Section 124 of the Transfer of Property Act, 1882, a gift comprising both existing and future property is","o":["void as to the existing property","valid as to the whole of the gift","void as to the whole of the gift","void as to the future property"],"a":3,"e":"A gift of future property is void. If the gift comprises both existing and future property, it is void only as to the latter, and remains valid as to the existing property."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"Under Article 324(5), the Chief Election Commissioner cannot be removed from office except","o":["by the President, on the advice of the Union Council of Ministers","like a Judge of the Supreme Court, and on like grounds","by the Supreme Court, on a petition of the Attorney General","by the Election Commission, acting through its other members"],"a":1,"e":"The CEC enjoys the same security of tenure as a Supreme Court Judge. Other Election Commissioners can be removed only on the CEC's recommendation, and the conditions of service of the CEC cannot be varied to his disadvantage after appointment."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Which of the following is NOT a requirement for the application of res judicata under Section 11 CPC?","o":["The matter must have been directly and substantially in issue in the former suit","The former court must be a court of higher jurisdiction than the later court","The former suit must have been finally decided by the court","The parties must be the same, or claim under the same parties"],"a":1,"e":"Section 11 requires that the former court be competent to try the subsequent suit, not that it be a higher court. The matter in issue must be the same, the parties must be the same, and the matter must have been heard and finally decided."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under the Probation of Offenders Act, 1958, a court may release an offender on probation of good conduct if he is convicted of an offence that is not punishable with","o":["death or imprisonment for life","fine of more than Rs. 5,000","imprisonment of more than two years","imprisonment of more than seven years"],"a":0,"e":"Section 4 allows probation where the offender is found guilty of an offence not punishable with death or life imprisonment, and the court thinks it expedient, having regard to the circumstances, the character of the offender and the nature of the offence."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"Under Section 102 of the Evidence Act (and its corresponding BSA provision), the burden of proof in a suit lies on the person who","o":["has filed the suit, in every case, without any exception","would fail if no evidence at all were given on either side","has the greater financial strength to bear the costs of the suit","is nearer to the facts, and has greater knowledge of them"],"a":1,"e":"The initial burden lies on the party who would fail if no evidence were led. The burden may shift during the trial, but the legal burden of proof remains on the party asserting the fact. Section 101 states the general rule."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"Under Article 61 of the Constitution, a resolution to impeach the President for violation of the Constitution must be passed by a majority of","o":["a majority of the total membership of both Houses sitting jointly","not less than two-thirds of the members present and voting","a simple majority of the members present and voting in the House","not less than two-thirds of the total membership of the House"],"a":3,"e":"A charge must be preferred by a House after a notice signed by at least one-fourth of its total members, with fourteen days' notice. It must be passed by two-thirds of the total membership of that House, and then investigated by the other House."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"In Pankaj Bansal v Union of India (2023), the Supreme Court held that the grounds of arrest must be communicated to the arrested person","o":["only if he asks for them before the Magistrate","orally only, at the time of the arrest","in writing, as a matter of course","to his counsel alone, after the first remand"],"a":2,"e":"The Court held that, to give meaning to the right under Article 22(1), the grounds of arrest must be informed in writing as a matter of course and without exception. Failure to do so renders the arrest and remand illegal."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Which of the following is excluded from the definition of 'decree' in Section 2(2) CPC?","o":["A preliminary decree passed in a suit by the court of first instance","The rejection of a plaint by the court under Order VII Rule 11","Any order of dismissal of a suit for default of appearance","The determination of any question under Section 144 of the Code"],"a":2,"e":"A decree includes the rejection of a plaint and determination of a question under Section 144 (restitution). It does not include an adjudication from which an appeal lies as an appeal from an order, or any order of dismissal for default."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Under Section 82(1) of the BNS, the offence of bigamy is punishable with imprisonment up to","o":["three years, and fine","ten years, and fine","five years, and fine","seven years, and fine"],"a":3,"e":"Section 82 BNS corresponds to Section 494 IPC. Marrying again during the lifetime of a spouse is punishable with up to seven years and fine. The second marriage is void."},{"s":"Taxation","q":"Under the CGST Act, 2017, the aggregate turnover threshold for compulsory registration of a supplier of goods in a normal category State is","o":["Rs. 10 lakh in a financial year","Rs. 20 lakh in a financial year","Rs. 75 lakh in a financial year","Rs. 40 lakh in a financial year"],"a":3,"e":"Suppliers of goods whose aggregate turnover exceeds Rs. 40 lakh (Rs. 20 lakh for services, and lower limits for special category States) must register. Registration is compulsory irrespective of turnover for inter-State supplies of services and for certain categories of persons."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"Under Section 60 of the Evidence Act (and its corresponding BSA provision), oral evidence must, in all cases whatever, be","o":["given on oath by the parties to the case, and not by any other witness","in writing, and signed by the witness, before it is received by the court","direct, i.e., given by a witness who saw, heard or perceived the fact","hearsay, if the person who gave the information to the witness is untraceable"],"a":2,"e":"The rule excludes hearsay. If the fact could be seen, the evidence must be that of a witness who says he saw it. Exceptions are provided for dying declarations and statements made by persons who cannot be called as witnesses."},{"s":"Contract, Specific Relief & Sale of Goods","q":"Under Section 138 of the Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881, after receipt of a demand notice, the drawer of a dishonoured cheque must make payment within","o":["forty-five days of the receipt of the notice","fifteen days of the receipt of the notice","thirty days of the receipt of the notice","seven days of the receipt of the notice"],"a":1,"e":"The payee must present the cheque within its validity (three months), give a demand notice within 30 days of receiving information of dishonour, and the drawer has 15 days from receipt to pay. If he fails, the offence is complete and a complaint may be filed within one month."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"In criminal law, preparation to commit an offence is generally","o":["not punishable, except where the law makes preparation for a particular offence punishable","punishable only if the offence that was intended is punishable with death","punishable only if the accused has procured a weapon for committing the offence","punishable as an attempt in every case, once the intention to commit it is proved"],"a":0,"e":"Preparation is not punishable in general, since it is the stage before the commission of a direct act towards the offence. The law makes preparation punishable only for specific offences, such as waging war, counterfeiting coin and making preparation to commit dacoity."},{"s":"Intellectual Property Law","q":"Under the Designs Act, 2000, the registration of a design is valid for an initial period of","o":["fifteen years, extendable by a further period of ten years","twenty years, with no extension that is permitted","ten years, extendable by a further period of five years","five years, extendable by a further period of five years"],"a":2,"e":"A registered design has copyright for ten years from the date of registration. It may be extended for a further five years on an application made before the expiry of the first ten years, making a total of fifteen years."},{"s":"Tort & Consumer Law","q":"Under Section 69 of the Consumer Protection Act, 2019, a complaint must be filed within","o":["five years from the date on which the cause of action has arisen","two years from the date on which the cause of action has arisen","one year from the date on which the cause of action has arisen","three years from the date on which the cause of action has arisen"],"a":1,"e":"The District, State or National Commission may entertain a complaint after two years if the complainant satisfies it that he had sufficient cause for not filing it within that period, and records its reasons."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"In Nilabati Behera v State of Orissa (1993), the Supreme Court held that compensation for custodial death can be awarded under Articles 32 and 226 as","o":["a punishment on the State, which can be imposed only after a criminal trial","a private law remedy for tort, which requires a civil suit to be filed first","a public law remedy for violation of fundamental rights, besides any private remedy","a form of relief available only if the victim's family has filed an FIR"],"a":2,"e":"The Court held that the defence of sovereign immunity is not available in such cases, and compensation is a remedy under public law. It is in addition to any other remedy, such as a civil suit or criminal prosecution."},{"s":"Tort & Consumer Law","q":"In State of Rajasthan v Vidyawati (1962), the Supreme Court held that the State is","o":["vicariously liable for torts of its servants committed in the course of employment","immune from liability in all cases, on the ground of sovereign immunity","liable only for the breach of contracts that it has entered into with others","liable only if the servant has been convicted of a criminal offence for the act"],"a":0,"e":"In Vidyawati, the State was held liable for the negligent driving of a Government jeep driver, since the act was not done in the exercise of sovereign powers. The Court distinguished Kasturi Lal v State of U.P. (1965), where the act was in the exercise of sovereign functions."},{"s":"Labour Law","q":"Under the Industrial Relations Code, 2020, the compensation payable to a worker on retrenchment is","o":["one month's average pay for every year, irrespective of the length of service","thirty days' average pay for every completed year of continuous service","fifteen days' average pay for every completed year of service, or part over six months","seven days' average pay for every completed year of continuous service"],"a":2,"e":"In addition to one month's notice or wages in lieu, the worker is entitled to retrenchment compensation at the rate of 15 days' average pay for every completed year of continuous service or any part thereof in excess of six months. The Code also creates a re-skilling fund for retrenched workers."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under Section 357A of the CrPC (now Section 396 of the BNSS), the Victim Compensation Scheme is prepared by","o":["Parliament, by enacting a separate law on victim compensation","the State Government, in coordination with the Central Government","the District Magistrate, in coordination with the Superintendent of Police","the High Court, in coordination with the State Legal Services Authority"],"a":1,"e":"Every State must prepare a scheme, in coordination with the Central Government, to provide funds for compensation to victims or their dependants who have suffered loss or injury due to a crime. The District Legal Services Authority decides the quantum."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under Section 14 of the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955, a petition for divorce cannot ordinarily be presented before the expiry of","o":["three years from the date of the marriage","six months from the date of the marriage","one year from the date of the marriage","two years from the date of the marriage"],"a":2,"e":"Section 14 bars a petition within one year of marriage, unless the court permits it on the ground of exceptional hardship to the petitioner or exceptional depravity on the part of the respondent."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Order II Rule 2 CPC, a plaintiff who omits to sue for a portion of his claim arising from the same cause of action","o":["cannot afterwards sue for the portion so omitted","may afterwards sue for it only after waiting for twelve years","is barred only where the omitted portion exceeds half of the claim","may afterwards sue for it as of right, without any leave of the court"],"a":0,"e":"Order II Rule 2 requires the plaintiff to include the whole claim arising from the cause of action. If he intentionally relinquishes or omits any portion, he cannot later sue for it, unless he has obtained leave of the court at the time of the first suit."},{"s":"Environmental Law","q":"The National Biodiversity Authority, established under the Biological Diversity Act, 2002, is headquartered at","o":["Kolkata","Chennai","Bhopal","New Delhi"],"a":1,"e":"The NBA, a statutory body, regulates access to biological resources and associated knowledge, and the sharing of benefits arising from their use. Its headquarters are in Chennai."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Under Section 9 of the Advocates Act, 1961, a Disciplinary Committee of a State Bar Council consists of","o":["three persons: two elected Council members and one co-opted advocate","three persons, all of whom are members nominated by the Advocate General","five persons, all of whom are retired Judges of the High Court of the State","seven persons, of whom four are appointed by the Central Government"],"a":0,"e":"Section 9 provides for Disciplinary Committees of three persons, two elected by the Council from its members and one co-opted from advocates on the electoral roll who is qualified to be a member, the senior-most being the chairman."},{"s":"Public International Law","q":"Under Article 34 of the Statute of the International Court of Justice, only which of the following may be parties in cases before the Court?","o":["States and individuals","States and multinational corporations","States","States and international organisations"],"a":2,"e":"In contentious cases, only States may be parties. International organisations and individuals have no standing, though the Court may give advisory opinions at the request of certain UN organs and specialised agencies."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Under the Bar Council of India Rules, an advocate shall not","o":["argue a case before a Judge who was once a colleague at the Bar","discuss a pending case with the client in the privacy of his chambers","consult a senior colleague on the legal points involved in the case","communicate privately with a Judge concerning a case pending before him"],"a":3,"e":"The Rules on duty to the court bar an advocate from influencing the decision of a court by any illegal or improper means, and from communicating privately with a Judge concerning a pending case. All communications must be in open court or in the presence of the opposite party."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Order XIV Rule 2 CPC, a court may try as a preliminary issue","o":["any issue relating to the relief claimed by the plaintiff in the plaint filed","an issue relating to the court's jurisdiction or a bar to the suit created by law","any issue on which the plaintiff has filed an affidavit before the court","any issue of fact which the defendant considers to be vital to his defence"],"a":1,"e":"Under Rule 2(2), as substituted in 1976, the court may try an issue relating to jurisdiction or a bar to the suit created by any law for the time being in force, if it is an issue of law, and only after it has been framed with the other issues."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"Article 39A, which directs the State to secure equal justice and provide free legal aid, was inserted by the","o":["42nd Amendment, 1976","44th Amendment, 1978","52nd Amendment, 1985","86th Amendment, 2002"],"a":0,"e":"The 42nd Amendment inserted Article 39A in Part IV (Directive Principles). The Legal Services Authorities Act, 1987 gives effect to it. Free legal aid has also been held to be part of the right under Article 21 (Hussainara Khatoon)."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Under Section 22 of the BNS (earlier Section 84 IPC), a person of unsound mind is exempted from criminal liability if, at the time of the act, he is incapable of knowing","o":["the consequences which the act is likely to have for himself","the nature of the act, or that it is wrong or contrary to law","the penalty which the law prescribes for the act done by him","the identity of the person against whom the act is done"],"a":1,"e":"The test is based on the M'Naghten rules, which look to the cognitive capacity of the accused at the time of the act. Legal insanity is different from medical insanity, and the burden of proving it lies on the accused."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"A proclamation under Section 84 of the BNSS (earlier Section 82 CrPC) must require the absconding person to appear within a period of not less than","o":["fifteen days from the date of its publication","thirty days from the date of its publication","sixty days from the date of its publication","seven days from the date of its publication"],"a":1,"e":"The proclamation must be published in a specified manner and require the person to appear at a specified place and time, not less than 30 days from the date of publication. A person who does not appear may be declared a proclaimed offender."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"An appeal as of right under Section 19 of the Contempt of Courts Act, 1971 lies against","o":["an order refusing to initiate proceedings for contempt of court","an order declining to punish the alleged contemnor after a hearing","an order issuing a notice to show cause to the alleged contemnor","an order of the High Court punishing a person for contempt"],"a":3,"e":"Section 19 provides an appeal as of right from an order of the High Court punishing for contempt. There is no such appeal against an order refusing to punish or an order merely initiating proceedings. In those cases, a special leave petition may be filed."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Under Section 15 of the Contempt of Courts Act, 1971, a private person may move the Supreme Court to initiate proceedings for criminal contempt only with","o":["the written permission of the Chief Justice of India personally","the written consent of the Attorney General or Solicitor General","a certificate from the Bar Council of India in his favour","a resolution passed by the Supreme Court Bar Association"],"a":1,"e":"Section 15 allows the Supreme Court or High Court to act on its own motion, or on a motion by the Attorney General, Solicitor General or Advocate General. Any other person needs the written consent of the Attorney General or Solicitor General (or the Advocate General, in the High Court)."},{"s":"Intellectual Property Law","q":"Under the Patents Act, 1970, a pre-grant opposition to an application for a patent may be filed by","o":["any person, after publication of the application and before grant of the patent","only a person who has already been granted a patent in the same field","only a competitor of the applicant, within three months after the grant of it","only the Central Government, acting through the Controller of Patents"],"a":0,"e":"Under Section 25(1), any person may make a representation by way of opposition after the publication of the application and before the grant. After the grant, an opposition may be filed within one year of publication of the grant, under Section 25(2)."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"Under Section 116 of the Evidence Act (and its corresponding BSA provision), a tenant of immovable property is estopped from denying","o":["the right of the landlord to evict him after the end of the lease","the validity of the rent which the landlord has demanded from him","the landlord's title at any time during the tenancy or after its end","the title of his landlord at the beginning of the tenancy"],"a":3,"e":"A tenant, during the continuance of the tenancy, cannot deny that the landlord had title at its commencement. He may, however, show that the landlord's title has since come to an end, and he can plead eviction by a paramount title holder."},{"s":"Property Law","q":"In a simple mortgage under Section 58(b) of the Transfer of Property Act, 1882, the mortgagee has the right, on default, to","o":["foreclose the mortgage without the intervention of any court","retain the title deeds and sell the property by private contract","cause the mortgaged property to be sold through the court","take possession of the property and receive its rents and profits"],"a":2,"e":"In a simple mortgage, possession is not delivered, and the mortgagor binds himself personally to pay. If he fails, the mortgagee has the right to cause the property to be sold through the court, and the proceeds applied in payment of the debt (Section 67)."},{"s":"Cyber Law","q":"In People's Union for Civil Liberties v Union of India (1997), the Supreme Court held that telephone tapping","o":["does not affect any right of the person whose telephone is tapped","is permitted only by an order of a Magistrate made after a full trial","violates the right to privacy under Article 21, unless done with safeguards","is permitted without any safeguards in the interest of public order"],"a":2,"e":"The Court held that telephone tapping is a serious invasion of privacy, and laid down procedural safeguards under Section 5(2) of the Indian Telegraph Act, 1885, such as the order of the Home Secretary and review by a committee. These safeguards were later incorporated in the rules."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"In appointing or declaring a guardian of a minor under the Guardians and Wards Act, 1890, the paramount consideration for the court is","o":["the welfare of the minor","the wishes of the minor's father alone","the religion of the minor's parents","the financial position of the claimant"],"a":0,"e":"Section 17 directs the court to be guided by what is consistent with the welfare of the minor, considering the age, sex and religion of the minor, the character and capacity of the proposed guardian, and the wishes of a deceased parent. The welfare principle overrides the parents' rights."},{"s":"Company Law","q":"Under Section 244 of the Companies Act, 2013, to apply to the Tribunal for relief against oppression and mismanagement, a company having a share capital must have at least","o":["one-fourth of the total number of members of the company in question","twenty-five per cent of the paid-up share capital of the company concerned","one hundred members, or one-tenth of the total members, whichever is less","a single member who holds at least one share in the company"],"a":2,"e":"Members may apply if they number not less than 100, or one-tenth of the total, whichever is less, or hold not less than one-tenth of the issued share capital, provided they have paid all calls. The Tribunal may waive the requirement."},{"s":"Contract, Specific Relief & Sale of Goods","q":"Under Section 34 of the Specific Relief Act, 1963, a court may refuse a declaration of right or status if the plaintiff","o":["is entitled to a legal character, or to a right to some property of his own","is residing outside the local limits of the court's territorial jurisdiction","being able to seek further relief than a mere declaration, omits to do so","is a person against whom the defendant denies his title to the property"],"a":2,"e":"Section 34 allows a declaration of a legal character or a right to property. The proviso bars it if the plaintiff, being able to seek further relief, such as possession or injunction, omits to do so."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under Section 2(i) of the Dissolution of Muslim Marriages Act, 1939, a Muslim wife is entitled to seek dissolution of marriage if the whereabouts of her husband have not been known for a period of","o":["four years","one year","two years","seven years"],"a":0,"e":"Section 2 lists the grounds, including that the whereabouts of the husband are unknown for four years, failure to maintain her for two years, the husband's imprisonment for seven years or more, and failure to perform marital obligations for three years."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"In Priyanka Srivastava v State of Uttar Pradesh (2015), the Supreme Court directed that an application under Section 156(3) CrPC must be supported by","o":["an order of the High Court in a pending petition","an affidavit of the applicant","a sanction from the District Magistrate","a certificate from the Superintendent of Police"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held that, to prevent abuse, applications under Section 156(3) must be supported by an affidavit, and the applicant must show that he approached the police under Sections 154(1) and 154(3). BNSS Section 175(3) now incorporates this requirement."},{"s":"Labour Law","q":"Under the Industrial Relations Code, 2020, a worker who is laid off is entitled to compensation equal to","o":["twenty-five per cent of the total of his basic wages and dearness allowance","seventy-five per cent of the total of his basic wages and dearness allowance","fifty per cent of the total of his basic wages and dearness allowance","the whole of his basic wages and dearness allowance for that period"],"a":2,"e":"Under the Code, a laid-off worker, other than a casual or badli worker, is entitled to compensation equal to 50% of the total of the basic wages and dearness allowance for the days of lay-off. The establishment must have at least fifty workers, in the case of the entitlement."}]};
